  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHeat builds through midweek as high pressure surface and aloft \nnudges closer to the area from the southwest and south. In the \nmeantime, there is still enough room ahead of the high for a \nmid-level short wave trough to graze northern portions of the \narea with at least sprinkles this morning, a few of which were \nnoted upstream. The exiting short wave may still be close enough\nfor isolated showers and thunderstorms this afternoon over \nnortheast portions of the area, and made an adjustment to \ncentral guidance to account for this.\n\nDespite sufficient instability, marginal/modest shear and slight\nheight rises should limit the severe threat this afternoon, but\nany downpour could be quite heavy, and lead to downburst wind\ngusts given DCAPE amid the mixed pre-storm environment\ncharacterized by the classic inverted-V temperature-dew point \nvertical profile.\n\nThe forecast remains mainly dry for Tuesday and Wednesday as \nshort wave troughs riding the periphery of the mid-upper level \nhigh traverse farther northeast of the forecast area. However, \nadjustments may eventually be needed if this corridor is slower \nto ease northeastward.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has shifted the Marginal Risk for Severe \nThunderstorms southwestward into far northeast portions of the \nforecast areas for the Tuesday/Tuesday night period, most likely for \na second thunderstorm complex Tuesday night tracking a bit south and \nwest of its Tuesday afternoon predecessor. A slight chance for \nthunderstorms has been added for northeast portions of the forecast \narea for late Tuesday, and its placement and timing may need further \nadjustment in subsequent forecast issuances.\n\nH85 temperatures peak around 20C this afternoon, and 21-22C by \nWednesday afternoon. This supports lowland high temperatures in\nthe mid to upper 90s F and, over dew points around 70F, heat \nindices could reach the lower to mid 100s F come Wednesday \nafternoon. We could see isolated instance of heat indices near \n100 F in the lowlands this afternoon, and then more common \ninstances in the lowlands, at least across the Kanawha Valley, \nTuesday. As a result, the need for heat headlines is likely to \nincrease through Wednesday and even continue Thursday, depending\nupon the timing of the approach of the next cold front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe ridge amplifies but retrogrades westward toward the end of \nthe work week, allowing s short wave trough to drive a surface\ncold front to and through the area from the northwest. This \nincreases the chance for / coverage of showers and storms and,\neventually, a break in the oppressive heat. The threat for\nstrong, heavy thunderstorms is possible amid adequate moisture\nand instability, and at least marginal/modest shear. Current \ntiming suggests the greatest threat for the bulk of the forecast\narea would be Friday afternoon.\n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed 15 percent risks for \nSevere Thunderstorms across northern and central portions of the\narea Thursday through Friday. The Weather Prediction Center has\nplaced a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall across northern \nand central portions of the area for Thursday, and then across \nthe entire area for Friday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModels concur on surface high pressure pressure building south\nfrom eastern Canada behind the cold front, with drier weather\nand more seasonable temperatures bringing a break in the\noppressive heat for the Labor Day Holiday weekend. It make take\nuntil after Saturday to completely shake the chance for showers\nand thunderstorms out of southern and eastern portions of the \nforecast area, as mid/upper-level heights build slowly east \nbehind the short wave trough, and ahead of mid/upper-level high \npressure large and in charge over the center of the CONUS.",
